#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125523} :activity/id #uuid "27341ce7-bad3-5506-8e90-5fea806b76fd" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125341 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125759 :tutorial/content "Let's now compare some properties of addition in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ \nIt turns out that the addition of two matrices in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is *very* similar to the addition of two vectors in $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Commutative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A1</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}A+B = B+A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Addition is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A2</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrices $A, B, C \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue}(A+B) + C = A + (B+C)$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Zero Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A3</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For the zero-matrix $O$ and any $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}),$ we have $\\color{blue} A + O = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Existence of a Unique Opposite Element:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A4</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ we can find the corresponding negative matrix $-A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ such that $\\color{blue} A + (-A) = O$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nAnd it's not just matrices that have additive properties similar to those of $\\mathbb R^n.$ Many mathematical structures do. Let's see some more examples." :tutorial/id #uuid "e21e1a72-75b8-5ae3-9b7d-96f08d2ffa86" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3184 :tutorial/title "Addition of Vectors"} :step/id #uuid "f746dfa7-8c4b-5575-86b0-34ab4d9d09e6" :step/math-academy-id 17452 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125342}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125342 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124297 :question/id #uuid "1a855d24-cb78-5398-89e1-3bfda6907f36" :question/math-academy-id "e-4787"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243920} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243929} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243932} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281463} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281464} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480670}] :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"} :step/id #uuid "22604921-be1a-5e8f-bdd7-8ac9e59c63a0" :step/math-academy-id 17454 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125521 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124400 :question/id #uuid "57bf5fb6-02e9-56a1-9641-342123f45406" :question/math-academy-id "e-8229"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124767} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125242} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281527} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508990}] :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"} :step/id #uuid "7b0d0289-9feb-5b9b-9cdf-8e8bea357afd" :step/math-academy-id 30785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125601}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125832 :tutorial/content "Let's also compare some properties of scalar multiplication in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and $\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$ It turns out that the multiplication of a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ by a scalar is *very* similar to the multiplication of a vector in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ by a real number. See the table below.\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\" width=\"700\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30\"><b></b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\mathbb{R}^2$</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"200\"><b>$\\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$</b></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A5</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot A = A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Scalar Multiplication is Associative:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A6</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta A) = (\\alpha\\beta) A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><strong>Distributive Laws:</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A7</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any matrix $A \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)A = \\alpha A + \\beta A$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr><td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">A8</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">For any number $\\alpha$ and any matrices $A, B \\in \\text{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (A + B) = \\alpha A + \\alpha B$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :tutorial/id #uuid "05cdf1b2-a823-5410-8df1-42fa4da19240" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4934 :tutorial/title "Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"} :step/id #uuid "605b2c91-28e4-589e-9ea6-93545635ab1c" :step/math-academy-id 30789 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125521}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125523 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125833 :tutorial/content "The structure $\\mathbb{R}^n$ is called a **vector space**. However, many other structures possess properties similar to $\\mathbb{R}^n.$ These other structures can be called vector spaces too. \n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn about some important properties used to construct vector spaces. We begin by discussing what it means for a set to be **closed** under addition and scalar multiplication.\n\nAs an example, consider the set $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ of all $2 \\times 2$ matrices with real entries:\n\n![](images/a5/a53ec7e9416ae5eee4a1408df358302567a5aa98a393d6ddcb19e861645d2317.svg)\n\nFor this set of matrices, we can define two operations:\n\n- the addition of two matrices, and\n\n- the multiplication of a matrix by a real number (called a scalar).\n\nNow, notice the following:\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under addition**. This means that the sum of *any* two matrices from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\n- $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is **closed under scalar multiplication**. This means that the product of *any* scalar and *any* matrix from $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R})$ is also a matrix in $\\mathrm{M}_2(\\mathbb{R}).$\n\nIdentifying that a set is closed under addition and scalar multiplication is an essential first step in determining whether a set can form a vector space." :tutorial/id #uuid "18653f19-b4cf-5f0f-ab39-b1ae33ecdc6b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4935 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "befa8020-1248-59aa-b092-5fd7f5b45c2e" :step/math-academy-id 30790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124432 :question/id #uuid "ff4fdeaf-bdb3-54d7-b5e9-2f3ad88b55a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9960"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050979}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125241} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440653} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186440655} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445223}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"} :step/id #uuid "78f1d4af-7528-5705-8b7b-224b627efd47" :step/math-academy-id 37506 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125341}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125860 :tutorial/content "It's important to realize that not all sets are closed under addition and scalar multiplication. Sets not closed under addition and scalar multiplication *cannot* be vector spaces. \n\nFor instance, let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients whose degree is greater than or equal to $5.$  \n\nWe note that $S$ is not closed under addition. To see why this is the case, let's define the polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ as \n\n$$\np(x) = x^5, \\qquad q(x) = 1-x^5.\n$$\n\nAdding the two polynomials using regular polynomial addition, we get\n\n$$\np(x) + q(x) = x^5 + (1-x^5) = 1\\notin S\n$$\n\nThe sum $p(x) + q(x)$ is not in $S$ because it is a polynomial of degree zero, which is less than $5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that our set $S$ is *not* closed under addition." :tutorial/id #uuid "b014039e-61b6-58d7-8f35-a72e6153c126" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6230 :tutorial/title "Sets That Are Not Closed"} :step/id #uuid "1402186a-b64d-5406-9767-99815ff7ecc1" :step/math-academy-id 37838 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125590}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125861 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we discussed several properties exhibited by vectors in $\\mathbb R^n$ and other sets.\nLet's consolidate these properties into a single list.\n\n| **Label** | **Name** | **Description** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| A1 | **Addition is Commutative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v} = \\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A2 | **Addition is Associative** | For any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v}, \\mathbf{w} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue}(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})+\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{u}+(\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w})$ |\n| A3 | **Existence of a Unique Zero Element** | For the zero-vector $\\mathbf{0}$ and any $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2,$ we have $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{0} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A4 | **Existence of a Unique Opposite Element** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ we can find the corresponding negative vector $-\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}$ such that $\\color{blue} \\mathbf{u} + (-\\mathbf{u}) = \\mathbf{0}$ |\n| A5 | **Invariance Under Multiplication by $1$** | For any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} 1 \\cdot \\mathbf{u} = \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A6 | **Scalar Multiplication is Associative** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\beta \\mathbf{u}) = (\\alpha\\beta) \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A7 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any numbers $\\alpha, \\beta$ and any vector $\\mathbf{u} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} (\\alpha + \\beta)\\mathbf{u} = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\beta \\mathbf{u}$ |\n| A8 | **Distributive Laws:** | For any number $\\alpha$ and any vectors $\\mathbf{u}, \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$, we have $\\color{blue} \\alpha (\\mathbf{u} + \\mathbf{v}) = \\alpha \\mathbf{u} + \\alpha \\mathbf{v}$ |\n\nWe'll meet these properties again in a future lesson, where we'll formally define what we mean by a vector space." :tutorial/id #uuid "72e9ea87-7b8e-5d00-821a-31813b203a2b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6231 :tutorial/title "A Summary of the Addition and Scalar Multiplication Properties of Euclidian Space"} :step/id #uuid "be30c408-bfbc-549b-b14c-3f8724059c1b" :step/math-academy-id 37839}] :activity/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124706}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426552} :activity/id #uuid "ad1fad64-3179-5550-8fbd-b2cacb8f6bb0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426552 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125219 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0df63851-c8fb-51b9-8279-b9ce973db4c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448744} {:answer/id #uuid "72568caa-ec38-5729-8b60-8a6b3e4875c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448745} :answer-field/id #uuid "cba615a8-a05c-5fd5-8da2-27773d6897c5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449412} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e82c59a4-3c39-5944-a925-1d794ff8047e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448746} {:answer/id #uuid "23723619-4476-5f35-911d-82bb0f2654bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448747} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e86d69e-421e-53ad-a7af-4aaf40021f6a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449413} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9158fa52-52e9-51bf-ab30-0f42a60a2390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448748} {:answer/id #uuid "a4485828-ece1-56e1-a680-c31afec40344" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448749}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186448748} :answer-field/id #uuid "3115521c-39e0-515f-a130-cb432836d5b8" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449414}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "73f62269-52b9-5341-87d2-165ee03b0821" :question/math-academy-id "q-74700" :question/problem "Let $U$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{3},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The zero-polynomial belongs to $U.$\n\n- **II.** For any $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n\n- **III.** For any $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in U,$ we have $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)=p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as \n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{3}.\n$$\n\n \nHence, $0\\in U.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in U,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider polynomials $p(x)={x}^{3},$ $q(x)=2{x}^{3},$ and $r(x)=3{x}^{3}$ from $U.$ Then: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x) & =[{x}^{3}-2{x}^{3}]-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{3} \\\\ p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)] & ={x}^{3}-[2{x}^{3}-3{x}^{3}] \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-[-{x}^{3}] \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $[p(x)-q(x)]-r(x)\\ne p(x)-[q(x)-r(x)].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "1f027f6f-3170-550b-8b2d-eb37f8a80677" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426556}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426553 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426551 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa6071c0-fff9-534e-9586-a01bcfff63d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453496} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fbd9b-de38-5c14-bd68-eddf37af75ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453497}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453497} :answer-field/id #uuid "868be7d6-6944-5cfb-b5c7-52473d4ca9b8" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454498} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "183add14-81ee-5c40-a875-870eb4d49183" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453498} {:answer/id #uuid "fcea62b3-8084-56ff-8cb4-1d53d269fe8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453499}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453498} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2b928b0-206b-5ef1-be69-8e64cd6dfcb3" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454499} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47cf2934-bf49-5738-8cbd-42bb2111933c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453500} {:answer/id #uuid "fadcb3d1-7cde-5c14-83fa-f208111194de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453500} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ded8d53-6371-5429-8b4f-32fe142e8588" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ca37d526-e95e-5c72-ad4b-d05af7166afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-162394" :question/problem "Let $E$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ that satisfy $f(0)=1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The exponential function $f(x)={e}^{x}\\in E$\n\n- **II.** For any $f(x),g(x)\\in E$, we have $f(x)+g(x)\\in E.$\n\n- **III.** The set $E$ is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\nf(0)={e}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\in E.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the function $f(x)=g(x)={e}^{x}\\in E.$ Then, \n\n$$\n(f+g)(0)={e}^{0}+{e}^{0}=1+1=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)+g(x)\\notin E.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $E$ is not closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3b196302-0187-5bfa-8880-a9d96466fbba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426554 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06b1f26e-74e3-54dd-9a19-c2d7237c25a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453526} {:answer/id #uuid "4b07ac45-b4fe-591c-9ec8-4c6f325efb4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453527}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453526} :answer-field/id #uuid "02495e60-1290-53ef-9f15-de2782ac8189" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454513} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e976187d-1a3c-5254-ac57-00139179499a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453528} {:answer/id #uuid "bca2a77d-be2d-5162-9b1f-b5aabfcce26d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453529}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453528} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b2dcd84-25cf-5410-9806-8fbaad74db0c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454514} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af1f792c-ea17-539c-b803-70174e0206c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453530} {:answer/id #uuid "00f766d5-13c9-54cf-8c6a-17327b8856ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453531}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453531} :answer-field/id #uuid "ded477a5-7ff9-5d55-972d-d2753c0b4f0c" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "42098bc3-a0a9-510c-94ee-f777802bb49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162666" :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $\\begin{bmatrix}{a}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ where $a\\in Z.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** For any $A\\in R,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot A\\in R.$\n\n- **II.** For any $A\\in R,$ we have $2\\cdot A\\in R.$\n\n- **III.** For any $A\\in R$ and $\\alpha \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +1)A=\\alpha A+A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example,the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\in R,$ since $4={2}^{2}$ and $2\\in Z.$ However\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\notin R,\n$$\n\nbecause a negative number ($-4$) can't be a square.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $R.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n2\\cdot A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\notin R\n$$\n\nbecause the $22$-entry of $2\\cdot A$ is not equal to $1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +1){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+{a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +1)A=\\alpha A+A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "652a43ef-f276-5ff0-8235-02448f8f97ad"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e2779fb-9930-5fcd-bcd1-7e1f1104d4a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450066} {:answer/id #uuid "91b3e3fc-389a-585f-96b5-7db78750f42a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450067}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450067} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f7c9859-71fa-5115-a310-1182f4bf63bc" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450987} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "662847ba-aeb6-5cbd-8649-2265e32224d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450068} {:answer/id #uuid "bf79e41d-5399-5862-8d64-f981b0f2cf1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450069}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450069} :answer-field/id #uuid "b083e9d7-fb4e-5c84-9a1a-a1c99a0f9d6c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450988} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69cd0a9e-9920-5b05-8ece-dcad3cae55ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450070} {:answer/id #uuid "dc7468af-0186-5e65-ab49-7c01cd3fc068" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450071}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450071} :answer-field/id #uuid "b050593e-e59b-5bdf-835e-4f773eafb358" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450989}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ff69a5c1-505a-5719-b6b9-7ec69e0931ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-162664" :question/problem "Let $Q$ be the set of all functions of the form $f(x)=a{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** There is a function $f(x)\\in Q,$ such that $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n\n- **II.** For any $f(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n\n- **III.** For any $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Consider the null function $f(x)=0=0{x}^{2}\\in Q.$ Then,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot f(x)=(-1)\\cdot 0=0=f(x).\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any function $f(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(x),g(x)\\in Q$ and any fixed $x\\in R,$ the results of $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "a1742ae5-ae23-562a-91d7-bbd1cf427a86" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426554}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426556 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360475 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc6bcc99-b3af-511c-91ae-c4f89185a51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452035} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2702cc-0039-5cff-ab51-3043f6c2744c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452036}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452036} :answer-field/id #uuid "649a81e0-ac77-5ec9-ae54-66f470930379" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452865} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b185955a-71e1-5cda-8cd9-a2fd6aaac544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452037} {:answer/id #uuid "38bb6b6b-c279-54ab-994d-20b82f110997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452038}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452037} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac94e2aa-cdd1-5e7c-b142-37d6eab761ea" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452866} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bf0ebb4-415a-54f5-9321-bd974e7046ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452039} {:answer/id #uuid "36b27cb9-c8ff-51e8-ad54-c9c26a9e4d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452040}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452040} :answer-field/id #uuid "27b49265-285f-512b-aebb-320dd01bc0ad" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d30a6a74-1882-58de-a0e8-caf74485928f" :question/math-academy-id "q-94700" :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** For any $A\\in R,$ we have $1\\cdot A=A.$\n\n- **II.** For any $A\\in R,$ we have $-1(-1\\cdot A)=-A.$\n\n- **III.** For any $A\\in R$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot {a}_{ij}={a}_{ij}\n$$\n\nand, hence,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $R.$  Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1(-1\\cdot A) & =-1(-1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =-1(\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)\\cdot 1 & (-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(-1)\\cdot (-1) & (-1)\\cdot (-2) \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 0 & (-1)\\cdot (-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =A\\ne -A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statements III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ea8e9bb5-f8d7-5d3d-83dd-b2ae2b6c3647" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426553}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186426537}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481695} :activity/id #uuid "81a6ec64-50f6-581c-9e4b-c943b6c901a5" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481681 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ddd351f1-32b0-542a-92ab-95f9a87f3a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453490} {:answer/id #uuid "d4b49d49-33d2-5666-b395-3b0958b349ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453491}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453490} :answer-field/id #uuid "20334df8-715c-597a-9335-d72197c17d35" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454495} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a4ec86e3-0b06-5acc-8fd7-8c9f8345e536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453492} {:answer/id #uuid "88ec95fb-679a-5210-89ac-59d13943115d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453493}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453493} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6115756-4217-5b2f-8bef-56b706a37a07" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454496} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1ad8b87-10f1-5c8c-acae-ea0d175d7cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453494} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd33e6e-78a5-5c6f-8605-3eefa3645da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453495}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186453495} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d221323-cdda-5fd5-bf5d-7b2bfe508d9b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ba8b444b-5956-57db-9cc4-5f6d4e3d8cd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-162390" :question/problem "Let $F$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $\\begin{bmatrix}a & a \\\\ 0 & a\\end{bmatrix},$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The identity matrix ${I}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $F.$\n- **II.** For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A\\in F,$ we have $A+O=A.$\n- **III.** For any $A,B\\in F,$ we have $A+B=B+A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, every element $A$ in $F$ satisfies ${a}_{12}={a}_{11}={a}_{22},$ however, for the identity matrix ${I}_{2}$ we have ${i}_{12}=0\\ne {i}_{11}.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, as for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ with real entries, we have\n\n$$\nA+O=A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B\\in F$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nA+B=B+A\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "1bae5018-694a-57d0-bb47-f584f45c64aa"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481694 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243957 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3497eda5-52bc-523b-945d-d05f19503f52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447802} {:answer/id #uuid "44ac635a-4bd5-5229-88e4-cbb7d8e96d5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447803}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447803} :answer-field/id #uuid "fdefdd82-0939-5e22-a509-768a1fed2432" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448941} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25aca01f-34fb-588b-9dbb-c11c047afdea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447804} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6a09c3-4c87-5a81-b7e3-0d9a6734a797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447805}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447805} :answer-field/id #uuid "418778fd-c7f4-5d7f-a3d0-fd03d98de531" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448942} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed7df062-5ec2-57b4-8536-99b55fed3f21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447806} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ea89e7-c9f8-55e0-ab34-178df16a57c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447807}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447807} :answer-field/id #uuid "68bf64ae-6cb2-5663-8dc1-b3784f64d81e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "43cc36f0-0278-53e6-b346-3f4ef69a0aaa" :question/math-academy-id "q-106812" :question/problem "Let $G$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)>0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** For any $f(x)\\in G,$ we have $1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).$\n- **II.** For any $f(x)\\in G$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha -\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)-\\beta f(x).$\n- **III.** The scalar multiplication of functions in $G$ is associative." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n(1\\cdot f)(x)=1\\cdot f(x)=f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha -\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)-\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in G$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta f(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )f(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "4d07ef2f-2cbf-5e7d-8015-0fbfa6b7a98e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481681}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481695 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480670 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48152817-d761-520e-9162-c1b4b1f93594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480231} {:answer/id #uuid "712e9086-50d5-5be4-8bd2-838841d9d6bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480293} {:answer/id #uuid "83d03ad1-699f-5271-aa16-12a88da19753" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480330} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e264d4-7eff-58c9-af68-052f1a966bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480388} {:answer/id #uuid "f6333884-91ca-5deb-b697-754d8fa83902" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480515}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480515} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ca2ea90-b950-5f61-9fa7-e9762d8343dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480542}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "750b7607-258b-55b2-8f3f-8eb360c8a574" :question/math-academy-id "q-94644" :question/problem "Let $V$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{2},$ where $a\\in Q.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. The zero-polynomial belongs to $V.$\n\nII. For the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in V,$ we have $p(x)+0=p(x).$\n\nIII. For any $p(x),q(x)\\in V,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the zero-polynomial can be represented as\n\n$$\n0=0\\cdot {x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, $0\\in V.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for the zero-polynomial $0$ and any $p(x)\\in V,$ we have\n\n$$\np(x)+0=p(x)\n$$\n\nsince $0$ is the unique zero element in the set of all polynomials.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(t),q(t)\\in V,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\np(t)+q(t)=q(t)+p(t).\n$$\n\nHence, the addition of polynomials in $V$ is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "4d72023a-93a2-5692-b6e6-e90b48d23afc" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481750}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481732 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243932 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61500ddc-eba6-520e-a887-b48bc3ff18f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447778} {:answer/id #uuid "5584b906-d37d-5f15-9ec6-b8942a06abd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447779}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447778} :answer-field/id #uuid "3644870e-0c2d-5ee1-a9ad-63af23683a5b" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448929} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60519578-6bbf-5170-bf83-14db96c9f9db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447780} {:answer/id #uuid "8a3fa0d0-82f8-5210-896a-59fc58a32a01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447781}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447781} :answer-field/id #uuid "733b49f6-094d-5040-86cc-dec4510b549e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448930} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "312dc01e-2c5b-55e3-94e8-5d2dbc027d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447782} {:answer/id #uuid "b41a99d3-c4b3-5fc7-a51f-b02c2727d79c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186447783}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186447783} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdbf6e1a-6a9e-5e00-b37b-25812a12873a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7c34661b-de46-5e42-88de-7fe1ed369bf4" :question/math-academy-id "q-106710" :question/problem "Let $C$ be the set of all polynomials $p(x)$ such that $p(0)<0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The zero-polynomial belongs to $C.$\n- **II.** For any $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x).$\n- **III.** The addition of polynomials in $C$ is associative." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $C$ contains only polynomials that evaluate to a negative number at $0.$ However, the zero-polynomial is *not* such a polynomial, as it equals $0≮0$ at $0.$ So, $0\\notin C.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\np(x)+q(x)=q(x)+p(x)\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is commutative.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in C,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)]\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "c6278aa4-8145-5f54-971e-38905bb6ea4f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481694}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481750 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1474a3e2-e736-5b19-b171-2993227de350" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452041} {:answer/id #uuid "9b98079f-fe90-5caa-80dc-7270db379b3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452042}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452041} :answer-field/id #uuid "1b953735-6549-5e74-ab38-2ec79639a675" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452868} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86ec80e9-0df8-5cfd-835c-fd48ded2c328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452043} {:answer/id #uuid "69a7ca61-3cc1-5e61-baf2-dce127884e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452044}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452044} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c2d98f2-45aa-5106-9dbe-0b4791dcbb31" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452869} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b9c42b9-1d20-519c-b18e-c231243717e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452045} {:answer/id #uuid "d0500db8-c267-5db0-b0d1-5a3a33dc76d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452046}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452046} :answer-field/id #uuid "a50fa798-62df-5307-a327-7013e9729e7e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0778fc80-bc7a-5da8-b455-9670cb2d5afb" :question/math-academy-id "q-94711" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+b,$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** For any $p(x),q(x)\\in L,$ we have $2(p(x)+q(x))=2p(x)-2q(x).$\n- **II.** For any $p(x)\\in L$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$\n- **III.** For any $p(x)\\in L,$ we have $1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, consider the polynomials $p(x)=x+1$ and $q(x)=x-1$ from $L.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p(x)+q(x)) & =2(x+1+x-1) \\\\  & =2(2x) \\\\  & =4x \\\\ 2p(x)-2q(x) & =2(x+1)-2(x-1) \\\\  & =2x+2-2x+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot p(x)=p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "fa635f78-df77-519a-8670-fb90693a201c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481732}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481574}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509026} :activity/id #uuid "2f4c21a1-9d38-5bd3-b500-c840f8fe443d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049737 :topic/id #uuid "0f66ea93-6fdc-5bbf-826c-7d07b2ef17d7" :topic/math-academy-id 1906} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c42ccdb6-b921-5da6-8d61-829338f9c0dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450078} {:answer/id #uuid "2b082b3a-de73-5d72-8407-3ea0f366430e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450079}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450079} :answer-field/id #uuid "92ded1d6-fd62-535d-a00c-5652c123a362" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450993} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b94594f5-40c0-57fa-a320-b6e74879d268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450080} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a33687-ca33-58d2-9263-d09f15df4540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450081}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450081} :answer-field/id #uuid "e22d5ab6-389d-5f69-99dc-1d75e06d9f38" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450994} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45057e96-393e-5a76-b084-845e711d890f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450082} {:answer/id #uuid "be6cad6a-51eb-557f-9298-7fa92791e5e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450083}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450082} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f084e17-158e-5d0d-b97b-c2a08151797a" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2124447c-ee22-58ee-9afe-87a9532907c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-162668" :question/problem "Let $Y$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices with determinant $1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** For any $A\\in Y,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot A=-A.$\n- **II.** For any $A\\in Y$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.$\n- **III.** For any $A\\in Y$ we have $2A\\in Y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in Y,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, we have\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot {a}_{ij}=-{a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=-A.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in Y,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta ){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\beta {a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )A=\\alpha A+\\beta A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\in Y,$ however $2{I}_{2}\\notin Y$ because\n\n$$\n|2{I}_{2}|=|2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}|=\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=2\\cdot 2-0\\cdot 0=4\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0c665127-012e-5722-82c0-c620231782dc"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509016 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6dc2795-4ab0-51de-a770-20d43c3a88f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450072} {:answer/id #uuid "06198632-4bef-5852-8df3-61b23cc71b43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450073}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450072} :answer-field/id #uuid "76c5b6a9-c201-5cfe-94b7-0a5d6e2baf31" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450990} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "554b7f4f-d75d-532f-af10-b13a3597fa28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450074} {:answer/id #uuid "39d14144-cd01-511c-b559-2f430b3240b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450075}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450075} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc461b09-0b94-5bf1-9079-3927e7dcb562" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450991} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "df5a4b91-36ef-5113-a4b9-d1f9b7d8da80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450076} {:answer/id #uuid "aa572e6a-aad9-513a-b665-99a85554378d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450077}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186450077} :answer-field/id #uuid "55832c01-3fed-5f96-9d33-cae8fdc270f3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186450992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "69e25145-91f1-58d1-afd8-be54a0c5863e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162665" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is at most $2.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\in S$\n- **II.** For any $p(x)\\in S,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot p(x)\\in S$\n- **III.** For any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $S$ contains only polynomials whose degree is at most $2.$ However, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is *not* a polynomial. So, $\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $deg(q)$ the degree of a polynomial $q.$ Then, for any $p(x)\\in S$ we have that $(-1)\\cdot p(x)$ is also a polynomial, and that\n\n$$\ndeg((-1)\\cdot p)=deg(-1)+deg(p)=0+deg(p)\\le 2.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1)p(x)\\in S.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x),q(x)\\in S$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the values $p(x)$ and $q(x)$ are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (p(x)-q(x))=\\alpha p(x)-\\alpha q(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "6fe086d3-2ba5-5ffd-ac7b-c7f62891a1d0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509002}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508990 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23a1c3e8-9ac7-5fd0-b915-254166ffbcec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508447} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7e6612-1d6f-535f-8078-00cc2ee22135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508532} {:answer/id #uuid "b13597ee-dc96-5f12-bf58-96c7d9dbf7cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508657} {:answer/id #uuid "b86ca311-5ec4-5f1a-90f3-2144f5d7ec2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508667} {:answer/id #uuid "cb2e1c85-661c-5c30-a579-2a791c364ae9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508710}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508447} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4fdc251-4753-54c7-b5d7-bf5f38519bcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e4e1e0d5-3773-5038-a8b4-577c1254dd92" :question/math-academy-id "q-106807" :question/problem "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ such that ${a}_{22}=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. For any $A,B\\in P$ and any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (A+B)=\\alpha A+\\alpha B.$\n\nII. The scalar multiplication of matrices in $P$ is associative.\n\nIII. For any $A\\in P,$ we have $1\\cdot A={I}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ and $B=({b}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha ({a}_{ij}+{b}_{ij})=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\alpha {b}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A+B)=\\alpha A+\\alpha B.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matric $A=({a}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta {a}_{ij})=(\\alpha \\beta ){a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta A)=(\\alpha \\beta )A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $P.$ Then,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\ne {I}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "8061fe35-5bb2-5a7a-a40b-747237b37284" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509016}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509026 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73a902ed-7491-5586-a972-d357f55a0986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452017} {:answer/id #uuid "e02d1c16-f579-53fa-a7d0-24fb41030a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452018}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452017} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf830ad1-3967-59b5-8125-48068d769bf7" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452856} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f9fc86f-cdfb-5892-81a6-1b0d9bc7eb9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452019} {:answer/id #uuid "e30cf1d6-9863-5658-be8b-c21ac4b2d15c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452020}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452019} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d0d49b1-179c-5765-84f1-7b10b986b831" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452857} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23886679-356d-53bc-88ed-fee5a9a5549f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452021} {:answer/id #uuid "2e29665f-db3f-5174-adca-366e367ba005" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452022}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186452022} :answer-field/id #uuid "e539d322-ecc4-522f-b82d-d2e18147e866" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7ff3e8b0-9d12-58ec-aef1-61a782559716" :question/math-academy-id "q-94649" :question/problem "Let $N$ be the set of all functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=0.$  Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The constant function $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ belongs to $N.$\n- **II.** For any $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we have $f(t)-g(t)=g(t)-f(t).$\n- **III.** The addition of functions in $N$ is commutative." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $N$ contains only functions that evaluate to zero at zero. However, the function defined by $f(x)=1$ for all $x\\in R$ satisfies $f(0)=1\\ne 0.$ So, $f\\notin N.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider functions $f(t)=2t$ and $g(t)=t$ in $N.$ Then:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t)-g(t) & =2t-t \\\\  & =t \\\\ g(t)-f(t) & =t-2t \\\\  & =-t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(t)-g(t)\\ne g(t)-f(t).$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any functions $f(t),g(t)\\in N,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(t)+g(t)=g(t)+f(t)\n$$\n\nsince the addition of functions is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "b1ceacff-cce5-592c-aeca-52a40311b2f1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509029}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509029 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124765 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "880bc7b0-9b8f-54fe-aa7e-fa379ad4a0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121188} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe55d0c-ed82-5a5c-8534-d7e8a0e6748e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121189} {:answer/id #uuid "c869a616-4948-5b17-bf40-26562dc6c06c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121190} {:answer/id #uuid "f0785868-764e-5e56-ab39-5ce7bc056805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121191} {:answer/id #uuid "45da7005-b050-5a0e-82bd-186b81039774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121192}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121190} :answer-field/id #uuid "ede7357c-07ca-51f2-85fc-45b77653a716" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186121187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "182848d0-78bc-53b7-9635-9bc3cbac2ccc" :question/math-academy-id "q-106686" :question/problem "Let $D$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}],$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $D.$\n\nII. For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A,B\\in D,$ we have $A+O=O.$\n\nIII. For any $A,B\\in D,$ we have $A+B=B+A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, every element $A$ in $D$ satisfies ${a}_{12}={a}_{21}=0$ and ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}.$ So, ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in D.$ Then\n\n$$\n{I}_{2}+O=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne O.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B\\in D,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nA+B=B+A\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "b3319bae-270f-56a4-8501-7d24a9dbec5a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186509017}}] :activity/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508380}]}
